1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,970
At sixteen minutes past ten, on the night of August

2
00:00:02,670 --> 00:00:06,390
fifteenth, nineteen seventy-seven, a radio telescope

3
00:00:06,090 --> 00:00:09,550
in Delaware, Ohio, was listening to a patch of sky

4
00:00:09,260 --> 00:00:12,000
in the constellation Sagittarius.

5
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:14,580
It had no moving parts.

6
00:00:14,670 --> 00:00:16,660
It could not point.

7
00:00:16,760 --> 00:00:19,110
It could only wait for the Earth to turn,

8
00:00:18,810 --> 00:00:21,590
and drag the sky across its beam.

9
00:00:21,690 --> 00:00:24,820
For seventy-two seconds, something in that patch was

10
00:00:24,520 --> 00:00:27,670
thirty times louder than the noise around it.

11
00:00:27,770 --> 00:00:29,580
Then it was gone.

12
00:00:29,670 --> 00:00:31,790
It has never come back.

13
00:00:33,610 --> 00:00:36,470
This is the story of those seventy-two seconds:

14
00:00:36,170 --> 00:00:38,980
the machine that heard them, the page that recorded

15
00:00:38,680 --> 00:00:42,200
them, the three days it took for a human being to notice,

16
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:45,020
and the half century of silence that followed.

17
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:56,250
The instrument was called the Big Ear.

18
00:00:56,350 --> 00:01:00,220
It was built at Ohio State by John Kraus and his students,

19
00:00:59,920 --> 00:01:03,110
out of wire mesh: a flat reflector a hundred and four

20
00:01:02,810 --> 00:01:06,190
metres wide, tilted to bounce the sky onto a fixed

21
00:01:05,890 --> 00:01:09,120
paraboloid, a hundred and ten metres across,

22
00:01:08,820 --> 00:01:12,180
which focused it onto one of two feed horns.

23
00:01:12,270 --> 00:01:15,300
Two thousand two hundred square metres of collecting

24
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,420
area: the sensitivity of a dish fifty-three metres

25
00:01:18,120 --> 00:01:19,490
wide.

26
00:01:21,110 --> 00:01:23,030
It could not steer.

27
00:01:23,130 --> 00:01:26,320
It was a transit instrument: the sky drifted through

28
00:01:26,020 --> 00:01:28,990
its beam as the Earth turned, and at fourteen hundred

29
00:01:28,690 --> 00:01:31,680
and twenty megahertz that beam was about eight arcminutes

30
00:01:31,380 --> 00:01:34,590
wide, a little narrower than the full Moon.

31
00:01:34,690 --> 00:01:37,980
Which is where seventy-two seconds comes from.

32
00:01:38,070 --> 00:01:41,030
Any source in the sky crossed that beam in seventy-two

33
00:01:40,730 --> 00:01:45,200
seconds, sampled six times, twelve seconds apart.

34
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:48,430
The telescope was not listening for a message.

35
00:01:48,530 --> 00:01:50,780
It was listening for a shape.

36
00:01:52,390 --> 00:01:55,250
In nineteen seventy-three it became the world's first

37
00:01:54,950 --> 00:01:57,350
full-time SETI observatory.

38
00:01:57,450 --> 00:02:00,590
Fifty channels, each ten kilohertz wide,

39
00:02:00,290 --> 00:02:03,460
centred on fourteen twenty point four zero five eight

40
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:06,440
megahertz: the emission line of neutral hydrogen,

41
00:02:06,140 --> 00:02:09,760
the most abundant atom in the universe, and the quietest,

42
00:02:09,460 --> 00:02:12,580
most obvious place in the radio spectrum to expect

43
00:02:12,280 --> 00:02:14,100
a deliberate signal.

44
00:02:15,700 --> 00:02:19,280
Every night, the telescope wrote itself down.

45
00:02:19,390 --> 00:02:22,590
A computer reduced each twelve-second sample to one

46
00:02:22,290 --> 00:02:25,970
character per channel: zero to nine for a quiet channel,

47
00:02:25,670 --> 00:02:28,090
then letters for anything louder.

48
00:02:28,190 --> 00:02:29,770
A for ten.

49
00:02:29,870 --> 00:02:31,630
B for eleven.

50
00:02:31,730 --> 00:02:35,720
All the way to Z, for thirty-five times the noise.

51
00:02:35,820 --> 00:02:38,990
The paper came off the printer in long fan-fold sheets,

52
00:02:38,690 --> 00:02:41,690
and every week, someone had to read them.

53
00:02:41,790 --> 00:02:45,310
By eye. Column by column.

54
00:02:45,410 --> 00:02:47,190
Row by row.

55
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:51,100
In the summer of nineteen seventy-seven,

56
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:54,180
that someone was a volunteer named Jerry Ehman.

57
00:02:54,280 --> 00:02:57,190
He was not looking for aliens that week.

58
00:02:57,290 --> 00:03:00,000
He was doing what the project did every week:

59
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:02,240
checking the instrument's paper for anything it should

60
00:03:01,940 --> 00:03:03,950
not have written down.

61
00:03:05,350 --> 00:03:09,270
On the nineteenth of August, three days after the observation,

62
00:03:08,970 --> 00:03:10,630
he found one.

63
00:03:10,730 --> 00:03:13,990
In channel two of a single row, a column of characters

64
00:03:13,690 --> 00:03:16,380
did something the page had never done.

65
00:03:16,480 --> 00:03:19,440
Six. E. Q.

66
00:03:19,150 --> 00:03:22,640
U. J. Five.

67
00:03:22,740 --> 00:03:27,760
A rise, a peak, a fall: six samples, perfectly symmetrical,

68
00:03:27,470 --> 00:03:30,040
exactly the shape a fixed source should make as the

69
00:03:29,740 --> 00:03:32,480
Earth carried the telescope past it.

70
00:03:32,580 --> 00:03:35,640
The U meant between thirty and thirty-one times the

71
00:03:35,340 --> 00:03:39,060
background noise. Thirty sigma.

72
00:03:39,160 --> 00:03:42,580
In a project that argued about signals of three.

73
00:03:44,090 --> 00:03:47,110
Ehman circled the six characters in red pen,

74
00:03:46,810 --> 00:03:49,570
and in the margin he wrote one word.

75
00:03:49,670 --> 00:03:54,020
Wow. It is three letters and an exclamation mark,

76
00:03:53,720 --> 00:03:56,400
and it is the most famous annotation in the history

77
00:03:56,100 --> 00:03:58,070
of radio astronomy.

78
00:04:00,480 --> 00:04:03,690
Three properties made it hard to wave away.

79
00:04:03,790 --> 00:04:05,990
One: the frequency.

80
00:04:06,090 --> 00:04:09,560
It sat within fifty kilohertz of the hydrogen line,

81
00:04:09,260 --> 00:04:11,830
in the narrow valley of the spectrum that astronomers

82
00:04:11,530 --> 00:04:14,830
call the water hole, the quiet gap between hydrogen

83
00:04:14,530 --> 00:04:18,150
and hydroxyl, where anyone, anywhere, might reasonably

84
00:04:17,850 --> 00:04:19,950
choose to broadcast.

85
00:04:20,050 --> 00:04:22,070
Two: the width.

86
00:04:22,170 --> 00:04:25,210
Natural radio sources are broad and messy;

87
00:04:24,910 --> 00:04:27,930
this one occupied a single channel out of fifty,

88
00:04:27,630 --> 00:04:30,000
less than ten kilohertz.

89
00:04:30,100 --> 00:04:32,230
Three: the shape.

90
00:04:32,330 --> 00:04:35,780
It rose and fell the way something in the sky behaves,

91
00:04:35,480 --> 00:04:38,310
not the way something on the ground does.

92
00:04:39,920 --> 00:04:42,510
And one thing that did not fit.

93
00:04:42,610 --> 00:04:46,240
Big Ear had two feed horns, aimed at two points a fraction

94
00:04:45,940 --> 00:04:47,840
of a degree apart.

95
00:04:47,940 --> 00:04:51,120
A source in the sky should have appeared in both.

96
00:04:51,220 --> 00:04:53,210
Only one heard it.

97
00:04:53,310 --> 00:04:56,000
Whether that was a trick of the beam, a fault in the

98
00:04:55,700 --> 00:04:59,270
receiver, or something stranger has never been settled,

99
00:04:58,970 --> 00:05:01,290
and the only instrument that could have answered is

100
00:05:00,990 --> 00:05:02,270
gone.

101
00:05:03,880 --> 00:05:05,690
So they looked again.

102
00:05:05,790 --> 00:05:08,970
For years, John Kraus aimed the Big Ear back at the

103
00:05:08,670 --> 00:05:12,030
coordinates: dozens of attempts, then hundreds of hours

104
00:05:11,730 --> 00:05:14,120
across the following decades.

105
00:05:14,220 --> 00:05:18,070
Nothing. In nineteen ninety-eight the telescope was

106
00:05:17,770 --> 00:05:20,930
dismantled, and the land it stood on became a golf

107
00:05:20,620 --> 00:05:24,400
course. The instrument that heard the only signal it

108
00:05:24,100 --> 00:05:27,050
could not explain no longer exists.

109
00:05:28,450 --> 00:05:30,310
Others kept looking.

110
00:05:30,410 --> 00:05:33,370
Arecibo, until it collapsed.

111
00:05:33,470 --> 00:05:37,300
Green Bank. The Very Large Array.

112
00:05:37,400 --> 00:05:40,580
The Allen Telescope Array, now sweeping millions of

113
00:05:40,280 --> 00:05:44,090
stars. All of them have returned the same thing:

114
00:05:43,790 --> 00:05:47,390
silence, at those coordinates, at that frequency,

115
00:05:47,090 --> 00:05:49,860
in every year we have pointed anything at that patch

116
00:05:49,560 --> 00:05:51,440
of Sagittarius.

117
00:05:52,860 --> 00:05:55,670
The explanations have grown clever.

118
00:05:55,770 --> 00:05:58,830
A reflection off a piece of orbiting debris.

119
00:05:58,930 --> 00:06:01,910
Interstellar scintillation, the galaxy bending the

120
00:06:01,610 --> 00:06:03,640
signal like a lens.

121
00:06:03,740 --> 00:06:07,510
In twenty sixteen, hydrogen clouds around two comets,

122
00:06:07,210 --> 00:06:09,730
until critics pointed out that the comets were not

123
00:06:09,430 --> 00:06:12,230
in the beam at the right time, and that the idea could

124
00:06:11,930 --> 00:06:15,060
not explain why one horn heard the signal and the other

125
00:06:14,760 --> 00:06:19,840
did not. Then, in twenty twenty-four, a serious candidate:

126
00:06:19,540 --> 00:06:22,980
cold clouds of hydrogen, briefly lit up by a magnetar

127
00:06:22,680 --> 00:06:26,160
flare, glowing like a maser at exactly the hydrogen

128
00:06:25,860 --> 00:06:29,840
line. The team found smaller, weaker cousins of the

129
00:06:29,540 --> 00:06:32,060
signal in old Arecibo data.

130
00:06:32,160 --> 00:06:35,730
Plausible. Not confirmed.

131
00:06:37,540 --> 00:06:40,620
Which leaves the coordinates: right ascension nineteen

132
00:06:40,310 --> 00:06:44,160
hours, twenty-five minutes; declination minus twenty-six

133
00:06:43,860 --> 00:06:46,680
degrees, fifty-seven minutes.

134
00:06:46,780 --> 00:06:49,670
Any dish on Earth can be pointed at them.

135
00:06:49,770 --> 00:06:52,950
Some still are, every year, by people who were not

136
00:06:52,650 --> 00:06:55,360
born when the paper came off the printer.

137
00:06:56,970 --> 00:06:59,980
The Wow signal is not proof of anything.

138
00:07:00,080 --> 00:07:04,140
It is a receipt: one page, one column, one word in

139
00:07:03,840 --> 00:07:07,030
red ink, and seventy-two seconds we have never been

140
00:07:06,730 --> 00:07:08,440
able to repeat.

141
00:07:08,540 --> 00:07:12,570
It might be a natural phenomenon we do not yet understand.

142
00:07:12,670 --> 00:07:15,310
It might be a transmitter that spoke once,

143
00:07:15,010 --> 00:07:18,830
and stopped. The honest answer is the same in either

144
00:07:18,530 --> 00:07:21,590
case, and it is the reason the search continues:

145
00:07:21,290 --> 00:07:23,090
we do not know.

146
00:07:23,190 --> 00:07:26,860
For seventy-two seconds, the sky was loud.

147
00:07:26,950 --> 00:07:29,840
Everything since has been us, listening.
